\section{Helmet constructions}\label{sec:S5}

\subsection{The fixed adult helmet and its placements}\label{sec:S5.1}

The centered placement puts each head's frame where the adult's lies in the sample recording (its device-to-head transform), keeping the ear line in place. The primary placement, top contact, raises the head from there until the nearest magnetometer coil center is << F.g3b_cfg_clearance >> mm from the scalp, the << F.g3b_cfg_dewar_spacing >>-mm Dewar spacing plus a margin. This best-fit assumption, not documented practice, favors Neuromag: a child in an adult helmet may be placed centrally and repositioned toward the expected source \citep{gaetz2008}, and a small child's shoulders can stop the head short of the top \citep{tesan2010}. The other placements bound this choice.

A family of << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> source-blind placements, chosen from the geometry alone, tests the sensitivity to placement: top and back contact, shifts of ±<< F.g3b_cfg_translation >> mm left--right and front--back, and rotations of ±<< F.g3b_cfg_pitch >>° pitch, ±<< F.g3b_cfg_roll >>° roll and ±<< F.g3b_cfg_yaw >>° yaw, each raised to contact (one lateral shift was infeasible for child C, leaving << F.g3b_child_c_n_family_feasible >>). Three further placements are the centered pose, the laterally centered pose (moved to the helmet's midline) raised to contact, and true contact at the Dewar spacing.

The adult's own $D$ was << F.g3b_adult_centred_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>, << F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >>, << F.g3b_adult_xcentred_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> and << F.g3b_adult_top18mm_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB at its measured pose, top contact, laterally centered contact and true contact, and it spanned << F.g3b_adult_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_range >> dB (<< F.g3b_adult_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >> dB) over the family, top contact ranking << F.g3b_adult_top_rank_in_family >> from the lowest. Against the adult placed by the same rule, the smaller heads' $\Delta$ was << F.g3b_smaller_centred_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>, << F.g3b_smaller_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>, << F.g3b_smaller_xcentred_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> and << F.g3b_smaller_top18mm_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB at the centered pose, top contact, laterally centered contact and true contact.

With sensor plus brain noise, the point estimates kept their sign against the gradiometers (<< F.g3b_smaller_dense_vs_grad_ib_delta_range >> dB) and the magnetometers (<< F.g3b_smaller_dense_vs_mag_ib_delta_range >> dB) and for the site-matched array (<< F.g3b_smaller_matched_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB). With the room field projected out, $\Delta$ was unchanged in the scaled adults (<< F.g3b_scaled_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_range >>, against << F.g3b_scaled_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB), lower in the templates (<< F.g3b_templates_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_range >>, against << F.g3b_templates_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB) and small in children A, B and C (<< F.g3b_child_a_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta >> << F.g3b_child_a_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_ci >>, << F.g3b_child_b_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta >> << F.g3b_child_b_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_ci >> and << F.g3b_child_c_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta >> << F.g3b_child_c_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_ci >> dB; child C's point estimate negative, its interval spanning zero). Their site-matched arrays gave << F.g3b_child_a_matched_vs_combined_proj_delta >>, << F.g3b_child_b_matched_vs_combined_proj_delta >> and << F.g3b_child_c_matched_vs_combined_proj_delta >> dB, every interval including 0.

The adult's placement spread exceeded $\Delta$ in << F.g3b_smaller_delta_below_adult_family_span_count >> of the << F.g4_n_smaller_heads >> smaller heads, and each child's own $D$ varied by << F.g3b_child_a_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >>, << F.g3b_child_b_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >> and << F.g3b_child_c_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >> dB (A, B and C) over the family. Against each head's family median instead of top contact, the difference of pooled medians from the adult shrank by << F.g3b_smaller_family_vs_top_shrink_range >> dB.

The OPM arrays were refitted to each head by the adult's rules (<< F.g3b_smaller_dense_sites_range >> dense and << F.g3b_smaller_matched_sites_range >> site-matched sites on the smaller heads; median standoff << F.g3b_all_dense_standoff_2dp_range >> mm above each head's own MRI scalp) and kept in every helmet, with sensor noise, room field and analysis band unchanged. The background variance per unit cortical area was calibrated once on the adult, so a smaller cortex carries less total background; as a sensitivity analysis, it was multiplied by << F.g3b_cfg_bg_factor_low >> and by << F.g3b_cfg_bg_factor_high >>.

Fit and head size act on $D$ through at least four routes: a wider SQUID gap and a shallower cortex raise it, whereas fewer OPM sites and, in the school-aged children, a near-adult cortex close to the OPMs (unverified near-scalp anatomy; Section~\ref{sec:S4.3}) lower it. The constructions below combine these routes differently, and none isolates one (main text, Section 3.5).

\subsection{The helmet scaled with the head}\label{sec:S5.2}

Every coil center is scaled about the head origin by the child-to-adult ratio of head circumferences, keeping coil size, orientation and noise, and the factor is raised in steps until no coil is within << F.g3b_cfg_dewar_spacing >> mm of the scalp; unlike the sphere's constant gap (Section~\ref{sec:S2}), the gaps scale with the head. The helmet was scaled about the centered head and, as a variant added after the first pediatric results were known to remove the templates' lateral offset, about the laterally centered head. The construction asks what the adult's fit would give at a child's size and is not a pediatric SQUID device; the main text treats it as secondary because it also brings the sensors closer than the adult's own fit (see below).

Scaled about the laterally centered head, $\Delta$ against the adult in its own scaled helmet was << F.g3b_smaller_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB, the highest upper interval bound, child C's, being << F.g3b_smaller_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci_hi_max >> dB (parcel-bootstrap intervals exclude placement and construction uncertainty). Only child C, whose helmet keeps a median gap of << F.g3b_child_c_cfx_gap >> mm against the adult's << F.g3b_adult_cfx_gap >> mm, was not negative (<< F.g3b_child_c_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> << F.g3b_child_c_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >> dB, an interval that also contains its fixed-helmet $\Delta$). With the room field projected out, the difference of medians from the adult was << F.g3b_smaller_cfx_dense_vs_combined_proj_d_minus_adult_cfx_range >> dB (<< F.g3b_smaller_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_cfx_range >> dB with sensor plus brain noise).

Scaled about the centered head, the helmet left residual gains in the off-center heads, the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month templates and child C (<< F.g3b_infant2yr_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >>, << F.g3b_infant18mo_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> and << F.g3b_child_c_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta >> dB; $\Delta$ << F.g3b_smaller_cf_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB overall). The OPM kept its absolute advantage in the scaled helmet ($D$ << F.g3b_smaller_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_range >> dB in the smaller heads), but the additional child--adult contrast is no longer established.

Three checks qualify this result. First, part of the drop comes from the reference: against the adult at top contact, the difference of medians was << F.g3b_smaller_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_top_range >> dB (child C << F.g3b_child_c_cfx_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_top >> dB, above its fixed-helmet $\Delta$). Second, the gap scales with the helmet and shrank below the adult's << F.g3b_adult_cfx_gap >> mm in << F.g3b_cfx_gap_below_adult_count >> of the << F.g4_n_smaller_heads >> smaller heads (<< F.g3b_cfx_gap_below_adult_range >> mm), a tighter fit than the adult's that favors Neuromag and that the helmet fitted at the adult's gap avoids (Section~\ref{sec:S5.3}). Third, the smaller heads carry fewer OPM sites (<< F.g3b_smaller_dense_sites_range >> against << F.g3b_adult_dense_sites >>); the adult's array subsampled to those counts lost << F.g3b_smaller_site_penalty_vs_combined_range >> dB, so at an equal OPM site count $\Delta$ in the fixed helmet would be << F.g3b_smaller_eqcount_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB.

\subsection{The helmet fitted at the adult's gap}\label{sec:S5.3}

The Neuromag helmet is scaled about the head origin of each head's laterally centered pose until the median gap from magnetometer coil center to scalp equals the adult's: << F.rev_cgap_adult_gap_mm >> mm, the adult's gap in its own helmet about its laterally centered head (scale factor << F.rev_cgap_adult_fitted_k >>), or, as a variant, << F.rev_cgap_adult_top_gap_mm >> mm, its gap at top contact in the fixed helmet. Coil size, orientation, integration points and noise are kept, and no coil center may come within the << F.g3b_cfg_dewar_spacing >>-mm Dewar spacing of the scalp or lie inside the head. In child C, this clearance binds first: the helmet stops at a gap of << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_gap_mm >> mm, << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_gap_above_target_mm >> mm above the target, with a scale factor of << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_k >> instead of << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_k_at_target >>.

Because the OPM arrays are those of the fixed helmet, the within-head contrast (the median over a head's targets of the target-wise difference between $D$ at top contact in the fixed helmet and $D$ in the fitted helmet) is a property of Neuromag alone, whichever OPM array is compared. Intervals use << F.rev_cgap_n_boot_primary >> parcel-bootstrap resamples for the dense array in the fixed helmet and in the helmet fitted at << F.rev_cgap_adult_gap_mm >> mm, and << F.rev_cgap_n_boot_secondary >> elsewhere. The fitted helmet (Table~\ref{tab:S5}) is a geometric control at the adult's median gap, not at its gap distribution, placement or coverage, and not a pediatric SQUID system.

