The within-head contrast, what Neuromag loses in a head to the fit, gap and placement of the fixed helmet together, was << F.rev_cgap_scaled_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_range >> dB in the scaled adults, << F.rev_cgap_templates_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_range >> dB in the templates and << F.rev_cgap_children_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_range >> dB in the children. The adult's was << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >> dB, because its laterally centered pose in its own helmet is looser than top contact. Child C's was negative although its fixed-helmet gap (<< F.rev_cgap_child_c_fixed_gap_mm >> mm) and clearance-bound fitted gap (<< F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_gap_mm >> mm) nearly coincide; at an equal median gap, the fitted construction itself is worth << F.wr_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib >> << F.wr_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib_ci >> dB in the adult, so this contrast is one of construction, not of gap.

The interaction, a smaller head's contrast minus the adult's, is the extra that the fixed helmet gives a smaller head beyond the adult: << F.rev_cgap_scaled_fitted_combined_ib_interaction_range >> dB in the scaled adults, << F.rev_cgap_templates_fitted_combined_ib_interaction_range >> dB in the templates and << F.rev_cgap_children_fitted_combined_ib_interaction_range >> dB in the children. At every vertex of a scaled adult it equals $\Delta$(fixed) − $\Delta$(fitted), but the area-weighted medians of these quantities are not additive (residual in Table~\ref{tab:S6}), so the within-head and interaction estimates are reported in their own right.

For equal OPM site counts, the adult's dense array was subsampled by farthest-point sampling to each smaller head's dense site count (<< F.g3b_smaller_dense_sites_range >> sites), the adult in the same fitted helmet. At the adult's gap, $\Delta$ was then << F.rev_cgap_smaller_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB: << F.rev_cgap_scaled_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB in the scaled adults, << F.rev_cgap_templates_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB in the templates and << F.rev_cgap_children_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >> dB in the children (the intervals of children A and B include zero). A smaller head's dense array thus gains a little at the adult's median gap and equal site count, possibly, though these controls do not test it, because its cortex lies closer to the OPMs. The fewer sites take about that much back in the scaled adults and templates and more in children A and B, where what else lowers $\Delta$ is not settled by these controls (provisional examples; Sections~\ref{sec:S4.3} and \ref{sec:S5.5}).

As a measure of placement uncertainty, $\Delta$ in the fixed helmet was computed at each of the << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> source-blind placements (Table~\ref{tab:S7}). $\Delta$ was positive at every placement in every head except child C. The crossed band is wider and includes zero in the children. Top contact was not an extreme of any band.

